Context: this is a draft of the existence part of Riemann's 'Theorie der Abel'schen Functionen' (1857), the Dirichlet-principle construction of single-valued harmonic functions with prescribed periods on a Riemann surface. The previous page treats the local behaviour of algebraic functions and their integrals; the next page treats the functions w, t, Pi and the representation Pi = gamma Pi_0 + sum alpha_k w_k + const. The page is written over a printed postal ledger form ('No 10', columns Auslage / Porto / Franco).

(1) Setup. T is the Riemann surface. T' is T cut by cross-cuts (Querschnitte) into a simply connected surface. One can obviously choose a continuous real function alpha(x,y) in T' with two properties:
- on each cross-cut it is larger on one side (the positive side) than on the other by a given constant;
- its Dirichlet integral Omega(alpha) = ∫∫_T (alpha_x^2 + alpha_y^2) dT, taken over the whole surface, is finite.

(2) Dirichlet principle. Vary alpha by functions continuous across the cuts. The page then asserts that there is a function u with three properties:
- it differs from alpha by a function continuous on T;
- it minimizes the integral;
- it is completely determined up to an additive constant.
As in Riemann's dissertation and Abel. Funct. §3, the minimizer satisfies three conditions:
- u_xx + u_yy = 0 in the interior;
- u has the same constant jumps across the cuts;
- the derivative normal to each cross-cut is equal on both sides.
The tangential derivative is automatically equal on both sides, because the jump is constant.

(3) Conjugate function. Since Delta u = 0, the expression (∂u/∂x) dy − (∂u/∂y) dx is a complete (exact) differential: d(u_x dy − u_y dx) = (u_xx + u_yy) dx∧dy = 0 (my explicit step). Integrating it through the interior of T' gives v, single-valued in T' and determined up to an additive constant. Because both the normal and the tangential derivatives of u agree on the two sides of each cut, the derivatives of v also agree on both sides. Hence v is continuous along each cut on each side and jumps by a constant: on the positive side of the cross-cuts v is larger by b^(1), b^(2), ..., b^(p). Then u + iv = w is a function of x + yi (an everywhere-finite Abelian integral, first kind). Its periods have prescribed real parts (the jumps of u) and imaginary parts b^(nu) that are determined by the construction.

(4) Several crossed-out attempts follow ('Jede ... in der Begrenzung von T' stetige Function', 'Und es kann für die Begrenzung von T' ...'). Then a fresh start generalizes the construction. In T one takes a function alpha of x, y which:
- in the neighbourhoods of given points has prescribed singular parts (from the previous page: a_nu log(z−s_nu) + b_nu/(z−s_nu) + c/(z−s_nu)^2 + ..., with z−s replaced by (z−s)^{1/mu} at branch points and by 1/z at infinity);
- elsewhere has a derivative that remains continuous or becomes discontinuous across lines in a given way.
The same minimization then yields functions with prescribed discontinuities: integrals of the second and third kind, t and Pi, as on the next page.
